Victory, O Mother! You who love to dwell in the enchanting grove of <s>kadamba</s> trees, like the wish-fulfilling tree, in the midst of the <s>bilva</s> forest growing on the jewel-island that rises within the ocean of nectar; you who are dear to the Wearer of Hides (<s>Kṛttivāsas</s> / <s>Śiva</s>), dear to all the worlds;
you whose three braids are adorned with a garland of <s>nīpa</s> (kadamba) flowers swaying in the eagerness of joyfully begun musical celebration, O daughter of the mountain;
you who are beautified by the row of smooth dark locks bound by the cluster of rays of the crescent moon that has become your crest-jewel, O one honored by the world;
you whose eyes create doubt whether they are the flower-clusters of the creeper-brows resembling the playful bow of <s>Kāma</s> (the god of love), O one who sprinkles the nectar of speech;
beautiful with the charming adornment of a forehead mark of lovely yellow <s>gorocanā</s> (orpiment) paste, O delightful one, O <s>Ramā</s>;
you whose voice has the deep resonance of a stringed instrument, enriched by the singing of the female bees attracted by the fragrance emanating from the musk-leaf patterns painted on the temples whose beauty is illumined by the halo of moonlight-like rows of pearl earrings gleaming brilliantly; O one of sweet voice, O radiant one;
you who are distinguished by earring-ornaments fashioned from <s>tālī</s>-palm leaves swaying in the manner of playing the <s>vallakī</s> (lute), O one honored by the <s>siddhas</s> (perfected beings);

You whose neck bears the beauty of a conch, born from the milk-ocean of loveliness overflowing with the rising moon of fresh youthfulness that has charmingly begun; O abode of fine arts, O gracefully slow one;
you whose faultless limbs shine forth, resplendent with the brilliance of divine gem-studded necklaces and other ornaments beautifully arrayed; O auspicious one;
you whose arm-creepers are adorned by the shooting rays of jeweled armlets, worshipped by <s>yogīs</s>;
you adorned with bracelets sparkling with the radiance of rubies pervading all the quarters of the world, adorned with graceful motion, honored by <s>sādhakas</s> (practitioners);
your pair of hands, rivaling the lotuses blossoming at the onset of day, constantly bestowing, non-dual;
you whose finger-shoots are illumined by the halo of nail-crescents rising from the glow of clusters of rays from divine gem-rings; O one with a radiant circle, in whom <s>cit</s> (consciousness) has its luminous sphere, with gleaming earrings;
you whose beautiful, ample bosom, smiling with rows of star-like pearl garlands, whose slender waist-creeper bends under the burden of the breasts, revealing the beauty of form in the waves of the fold-lines — O treasure-house of beauty, O bearer of the <s>vallakī</s> (lute), whose hands make all attendants prosper;

You are sung by <s>Kinnaras</s> with a <s>vīṇā</s> whose tones are sweet, captivating to the ear,
you are worshipped by circles of <s>Yakṣa</s>, <s>Gandharva</s>, and <s>Siddha</s> maidens,
you are propitiated by all the brides of the gods, who desire every auspiciousness.
Caressing that parrot—whose recitation of charming verses embodies all learning, whose three rows of shining letters gleam at the base of his throat, whose two beautiful dark-green wings are soft and splendid, whose beak surpasses the beauty of the <s>kiṃśuka</s> (flame of the forest) flower—you play.
Holding in your two lotus-hands a crystal rosary (<s>akṣamālā</s>), a book that is the essence of knowledge, a goad (<s>aṅkuśa</s>), and a noose (<s>pāśa</s>)—whoever meditates upon you, from his mouth flows <s>Bhāratī</s> (speech) consisting of prose and verse;
whoever contemplates your form of lac-red hue, to him women and men become subject;
